WEBVTT

00:00:07.667 --> 00:00:09.833
[engine starts, roars]

00:00:19.567 --> 00:00:23.267
[Jerry] <i>This is a 1963 Corvette Stingray </i>
<i>in silver blue,</i>

00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:27.533
<i>with a blue interior</i>
<i>and kind of a dark purplish upholstery.</i>

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:28.433
<i>Killer.</i>

00:00:31.333 --> 00:00:33.533
<i>It's got a 327 cubic inch V8,</i>

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:36.633
<i>four-barrel carb,</i>
<i>and proper knock-off wheels.</i>

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.033
<i>Are there faster Corvettes? Yes.</i>

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.867
<i>Are there bigger-engine,</i>
<i>tire-melting Corvettes?</i>

00:00:43.933 --> 00:00:44.900
<i>Of course.</i>

00:00:44.967 --> 00:00:46.567
<i>Are there cooler Corvettes?</i>

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.100
<i>I don't think so.</i>

00:00:51.933 --> 00:00:55.333
<i>1963 is the only year</i>
<i>with the split rear window.</i>

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.267
<i>This car looks like a submarine.</i>

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.067
<i>Like you could drive</i>
<i>right into the water and keep going.</i>

00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:06.100
<i>It's edgy, it's stubby,</i>
<i>it's perfectly proportioned,</i>

00:01:06.167 --> 00:01:09.600
<i>with just the right amount of detail</i>
<i>and just the right stance.</i>

00:01:11.333 --> 00:01:12.533
<i>In the '60s and '70s,</i>

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.767
<i>all American astronauts</i>
<i>in the space program drove Corvettes.</i>

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:19.433
<i>When you drive a Saturn V at work,</i>

00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:23.033
<i>you cannot get out of that</i>
<i>and into a Country Squire wagon.</i>

00:01:24.367 --> 00:01:26.967
<i>Look at the scoops. Look at the nose.</i>
<i>Look at the tail.</i>

00:01:27.067 --> 00:01:29.900
<i>Look at the door cutouts into the roof.</i>

00:01:29.967 --> 00:01:32.967
<i>The idea behind this car</i>
<i>is just confidence.</i>

00:01:33.067 --> 00:01:36.800
<i>This came from an American culture</i>
<i>that would just say things and do them</i>

00:01:36.867 --> 00:01:39.167
<i>without having any idea how.</i>

00:01:39.233 --> 00:01:40.667
<i>"We're gonna put a man on the moon."</i>

00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:42.667
<i>"How we gonna do it?</i>
<i>When we gonna do it?</i>

00:01:42.733 --> 00:01:44.933
<i>Why are we doing this?" "No idea."</i>

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.867
<i>We just did stuff. Crazy, but great.</i>

00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:51.800
<i>And I think this cool blue</i>
<i>is just the right color</i>

00:01:51.867 --> 00:01:55.033
<i>for my are-you-kidding-me</i>
<i>super-special guest today,</i>

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:59.000
<i>the commander in chief</i>
<i>of the United States of America,</i>

00:01:59.067 --> 00:02:00.667
<i>President Barack Obama...</i>

00:02:01.633 --> 00:02:02.900
<i>who, in my opinion,</i>

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.633
<i>has gotten off just enough funny lines</i>
<i>to qualify for getting on this show.</i>

00:02:08.167 --> 00:02:10.800
<i>A few weeks ago,</i>
<i>Dick Cheney says he thinks</i>

00:02:10.867 --> 00:02:13.500
<i>I'm the worst president of his lifetime.</i>

00:02:13.567 --> 00:02:14.700
[audience laughs]

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:16.367
<i>Which is interesting, because I think</i>

00:02:16.433 --> 00:02:19.133
<i>Dick Cheney is the worst president</i>
<i>of my lifetime.</i>

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.200
[audience laughing]

00:02:20.567 --> 00:02:22.800
-[telephone ringing]
-[man, over phone]<i> White House.</i>

00:02:22.867 --> 00:02:25.133
[Jerry] <i>Yes, may I speak</i>
<i>with the president, please?</i>

00:02:25.178 --> 00:02:27.247
-[Obama]<i> Speaking.</i>
-[Jerry laughs]

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:32.367
[Jerry]<i> Sir, may I compliment you on</i>
<i>cutting all that red tape in Washington.</i>

00:02:32.753 --> 00:02:34.087
<i>So you still up for a coffee?</i>

00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:37.124
[Obama] <i>Sure. Why don't you come on by?</i>
<i>It's 1600 Pennsylvania.</i>

00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:39.533
[Jerry] <i>Yeah. I know.</i>

00:02:41.367 --> 00:02:46.500
<i>I'm Jerry Seinfeld, and this is</i>
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

00:02:54.733 --> 00:02:56.267
[Jerry whistling]

00:02:56.333 --> 00:02:57.467
[leaves rustling]

00:03:14.361 --> 00:03:15.328
[Jerry] You ready?

00:03:15.996 --> 00:03:17.464
I got some stuff to do.

00:03:19.733 --> 00:03:20.867
[Jerry sighs]

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:35.767
Are these washed?

00:03:35.967 --> 00:03:38.300
[Obama chuckling]

00:03:39.986 --> 00:03:42.189
Come on, let's go.
Let's go get some coffee.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:46.267
-Well, don't leave the apple, though...
-[Jerry chuckles]

00:03:46.359 --> 00:03:48.295
If you're gonna have a bite,
you've gotta take it.

00:03:48.395 --> 00:03:51.064
[Jerry] Okay. Can I throw this out?
You have a garbage?

00:03:51.167 --> 00:03:53.133
You have to use
the non-presidential garbage.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:54.133
Oh, I see.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.467
-[Jerry laughs]
-[Obama] Come on.

00:03:55.569 --> 00:03:57.704
[inaudible]

00:03:58.833 --> 00:04:01.367
-This is a sweet car right here.
-Okay, now check this out.

00:04:01.433 --> 00:04:02.467
This is beautiful.

00:04:02.533 --> 00:04:05.300
I thought, the coolest car,

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:06.600
American-made,

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:09.333
for the coolest guy
ever to hold this office.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:10.533
[Obama] I...

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:13.367
You know what? You are winning
a lot of points with me right now.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:14.888
[inaudible]

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:17.900
A friend of my grandfather's,

00:04:18.125 --> 00:04:21.161
who had this when I was, like,

00:04:21.294 --> 00:04:23.597
-I was what, seven or eight or something.
-Right.

00:04:24.133 --> 00:04:26.733
And I remember seeing this thing
and thinking,

00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:29.800
if I was ever able
to get me one of these...

00:04:29.867 --> 00:04:31.400
-Yeah.
-...I'd be set.

00:04:31.967 --> 00:04:34.000
-Well, let's hop in. Come on.
-Let's go.

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:35.600
I'm glad you had some free time.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:37.667
I really like this car.

00:04:44.933 --> 00:04:47.100
-Be careful about hitting, uh...
-Oh.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:49.100
-[Obama] You look nervous.
-I'm a little nervous.

00:04:49.167 --> 00:04:51.600
-Are you always this nervous?
-No, this is a little different.

00:04:51.658 --> 00:04:54.094
-Like with Fallon and Letterman?
-It's not the same.

00:04:54.167 --> 00:04:56.333
-No. It's not the same.
-It's not as stressful.

00:04:57.397 --> 00:04:59.466
[Jerry] We're just gonna run out
for a quick coffee.

00:04:59.533 --> 00:05:01.601
-We'll be right back.
-I'm sorry. That's not possible.

00:05:01.667 --> 00:05:04.433
I do this little show,
<i>Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.</i>

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:06.400
You're a comedian with the president
going nowhere.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:07.933
-Back it up.
-Yes, sir.

00:05:12.467 --> 00:05:14.133
[Jerry] Do you ever think

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:19.033
about every person you talk to
is putting on an act, a total show?

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:21.167
-It's a problem.
-[Jerry laughs]

00:05:21.867 --> 00:05:25.200
Don't you think every American child
is a president fan?

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.733
You grow up loving the president.

00:05:27.794 --> 00:05:31.398
I do really well
with the zero to eight demographic.

00:05:31.464 --> 00:05:32.899
-Oh, really?
-Yeah. They love me.

00:05:32.967 --> 00:05:35.300
-Partly 'cause I think my ears are big.
-[Jerry laughing]

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:37.767
And so I look
a little like a cartoon character.

00:05:37.804 --> 00:05:41.741
-Right.
-And then little kids love saying my name.

00:05:42.167 --> 00:05:44.733
-Right. That's funny.
-But it's all one big name.

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:46.200
It's Barackobama.

00:05:46.267 --> 00:05:49.533
[Jerry] It is one of those names
that you could compress into one word.

00:05:49.583 --> 00:05:51.117
-One word. Barackobama.
-It's good.

00:05:51.184 --> 00:05:52.786
It's like a Nickelodeon thing.

00:05:52.867 --> 00:05:55.000
[Jerry] Well, anything "orama" is fun.

00:05:55.067 --> 00:05:56.400
[Obama laughing]

00:05:56.967 --> 00:05:59.100
This is called The Beast.

00:05:59.733 --> 00:06:03.233
It's a Caddy, basically on a tank frame.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.767
-[Jerry] Oh, my God.
-Yeah, see?

00:06:06.833 --> 00:06:10.203
So, I could call a nuclear submarine
right here from this...

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:12.539
-I'll bet you don't have that in your car.
-I don't have that.

00:06:12.633 --> 00:06:14.067
Yeah, it's a cool feature.

00:06:14.133 --> 00:06:15.367
Plus, seat warmers.

00:06:15.433 --> 00:06:18.533
-So, between the nuclear submarine thing--
-Well, everybody's got that. Yeah.

00:06:18.633 --> 00:06:19.967
Is that right? That's standard now?

00:06:20.033 --> 00:06:22.033
That's pretty standard
everywhere now, yeah.

00:06:22.133 --> 00:06:24.000
[both laughing]

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:26.400
This I like, though. I'm gonna get that.

00:06:27.433 --> 00:06:29.033
You can't have that.

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:33.533
What is it that you really want to do
that they don't let you do?

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:36.867
I would love to just be taking a walk,

00:06:36.933 --> 00:06:40.633
and then I run into you,
you're sitting on a bench. Right?

00:06:40.734 --> 00:06:42.102
-Now I see--
-And suddenly I say,

00:06:42.168 --> 00:06:44.638
"Hey, Jerry, how you doing?"
You say, "I'm doing pretty good.

00:06:44.704 --> 00:06:47.207
-What are you doing?"
-"Nothing. It's a Saturday morning."

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:48.600
Right.

00:06:48.867 --> 00:06:51.233
-That moment when you lost your anonymity.
-Mmm-hmm.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:56.067
And anonymity is not something
you think about as being valuable.

00:06:56.933 --> 00:06:58.533
-With all due respect...
-Yeah.

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:00.733
I remember very well not being famous.

00:07:00.833 --> 00:07:04.367
-You think it was okay?
-It wasn't that great.

00:07:04.433 --> 00:07:05.433
-Yeah?
-It was...

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:07.200
You think being famous is better?

00:07:09.433 --> 00:07:10.633
-Yes?
-Yeah.

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:12.433
[both laughing]

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:14.800
I always wanted to be
in a show about nothing,

00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:15.933
and here I am.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:19.233
There's nothing
more nothing than this. Nothing.

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:20.700
Come on, let's go get some coffee.

00:07:22.242 --> 00:07:25.211
[Jerry] <i>So, when you were a kid,</i>
<i>did you have favorite presidents?</i>

00:07:25.333 --> 00:07:27.967
Teddy Roosevelt is a cool character.

00:07:28.033 --> 00:07:32.933
In fact, he may be the guy who
would be the most fun to hang out with.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:35.767
He just does crazy stuff.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:38.300
[Obama] Mmm.

00:07:39.067 --> 00:07:41.733
-[Jerry] So--
-That's just a little, a little gin.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:43.133
-[Jerry laughs]
-I take midday.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:44.267
You were asking...

00:07:44.333 --> 00:07:46.100
You were asking what part of the day,

00:07:46.167 --> 00:07:47.467
-what part of the day--
-Yeah, when--

00:07:47.533 --> 00:07:49.900
-It's right about now.
-Yeah. That gives you a little zip?

00:07:51.333 --> 00:07:55.200
[Obama] Teddy Roosevelt would go up to
Yellowstone Park for, like, a month,

00:07:55.267 --> 00:07:58.233
and nobody knew where he was,
nobody could get in touch with him.

00:07:58.533 --> 00:08:00.800
-Can you imagine that?
-Wait a minute. In office?

00:08:00.867 --> 00:08:03.700
In office. Sounds pretty good to me.

00:08:03.984 --> 00:08:06.319
[Jerry] Boy, that's a lot of messages
when you get back.

00:08:11.533 --> 00:08:13.267
-How do you get up?
-[Obama] Uh...

00:08:13.367 --> 00:08:15.367
-Do you need an alarm?
-Somebody calls me.

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:17.033
You get a wake-up call?

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:18.200
Yeah. Why not?

00:08:19.566 --> 00:08:20.667
[Jerry] Get out of bed--

00:08:20.733 --> 00:08:24.767
So, I brush my teeth, shave,
put on some workout clothes.

00:08:24.867 --> 00:08:26.433
I go up, work out.

00:08:26.533 --> 00:08:27.967
Shave, then work out.

00:08:28.033 --> 00:08:30.500
Yeah, I believe in shaving
before the workout.

00:08:30.767 --> 00:08:31.600
Okay.

00:08:31.667 --> 00:08:36.500
Because that's how I do it,
and I don't really need a reason.

00:08:36.567 --> 00:08:37.433
[Jerry] <i>I see.</i>

00:08:41.033 --> 00:08:42.567
I guess I'll just make some myself.

00:08:42.633 --> 00:08:43.800
Let's see you make coffee.

00:08:49.467 --> 00:08:52.933
How far can you wander around up there
in your underwear?

00:08:53.067 --> 00:08:56.400
How far can you get before there's,
like, people and it's not cool?

00:08:56.967 --> 00:09:00.100
It's not cool, generally,
wandering around in my underwear.

00:09:01.267 --> 00:09:02.967
Can you really get a good night's...

00:09:03.033 --> 00:09:05.600
Isn't this like <i>Night at the Museum</i>
when you go to sleep here?

00:09:05.667 --> 00:09:06.500
First night was.

00:09:06.567 --> 00:09:12.367
Of all the jarring elements
of first being elected president.

00:09:12.433 --> 00:09:14.233
You know, you've got the inauguration,

00:09:14.287 --> 00:09:16.556
and there are two million people,
and there are parades,

00:09:16.623 --> 00:09:19.392
and there's this and there's that,
all the hoopla.

00:09:19.467 --> 00:09:24.400
The thing that really does get to you
is, after all that's over,

00:09:24.764 --> 00:09:30.236
and it's the first night,
and you're sleeping in the White House.

00:09:30.333 --> 00:09:32.167
-Right. Right.
-It's not like a hotel room.

00:09:32.233 --> 00:09:33.067
Right.

00:09:33.133 --> 00:09:35.000
So, you're looking around
and you're thinking,

00:09:35.067 --> 00:09:36.533
-"What the hell is this?"
-[laughs]

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:37.667
-Right?
-Yeah.

00:09:37.733 --> 00:09:38.833
<i>-Night at the Museum.</i>
-Yeah.

00:09:38.933 --> 00:09:41.800
That's how it feels
probably the first week.

00:09:41.867 --> 00:09:44.633
There's a lot of crazy stuff
that's happened in this house.

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:46.133
There is, yeah.

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:48.333
Does that ever enter your mind?

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:49.733
-Sometimes it does.
-Yeah.

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:53.067
There are times where you think,

00:09:53.159 --> 00:09:55.261
"Okay, here's where

00:09:55.328 --> 00:09:56.796
-Kennedy was hanging out."
-Right.

00:09:56.900 --> 00:10:00.033
Or "Here's where Nixon
was talking to Haldeman

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:02.633
-about whatever they were talking about."
-Right. Yeah, yeah.

00:10:03.036 --> 00:10:05.605
Can you adjust the temperature
in here at all?

00:10:05.672 --> 00:10:08.174
Is there a therm-- Have you ever
touched a thermostat in here?

00:10:08.241 --> 00:10:10.210
No, I make a call.

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:11.400
You make a call. [laughs]

00:10:14.867 --> 00:10:17.800
If I slid open your underwear drawer,

00:10:17.867 --> 00:10:20.367
one brand or a number of brands?

00:10:20.433 --> 00:10:22.967
-You gotta go with one brand.
-One brand. One color?

00:10:23.389 --> 00:10:25.625
Yeah, of course.
This is a critical concept.

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:27.667
It's more work that you don't need.

00:10:27.733 --> 00:10:30.500
How many suits do they put out
in the morning?

00:10:30.563 --> 00:10:33.633
Well, they don't put out suits.
I have a closet like normal people.

00:10:33.733 --> 00:10:35.067
-You pick the suit?
-I do.

00:10:35.133 --> 00:10:36.533
[chuckles] Yes.

00:10:36.633 --> 00:10:37.600
-Really?
-I do. I do.

00:10:37.667 --> 00:10:40.367
I would have a guy just leave the...
Same with the lunch.

00:10:40.433 --> 00:10:42.167
Here's the suit, here's the lunch.

00:10:42.233 --> 00:10:46.233
Well, with the suit,
I only have blue and gray suits.

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:48.267
-Right. No black?
-I have one black suit.

00:10:48.333 --> 00:10:50.633
Black's too rough for a president.
You gotta soften it a little.

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:53.167
-Well, but I'm a cool president, so--
-Right. That's true.

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:58.800
What's the coolest
president commemoration?

00:10:59.267 --> 00:11:01.333
-Oh, okay. That's interesting.
-To get?

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:04.033
'Cause you're in line
to get something great.

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:05.467
The Rushmore thing...

00:11:05.533 --> 00:11:07.333
-Rushmore?
-...would be interesting,

00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:10.633
but that's pretty exclusive real estate.

00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:12.467
You know what I noticed about
Mount Rushmore?

00:11:12.533 --> 00:11:16.100
Washington has totally turned his back
on those other three guys.

00:11:16.167 --> 00:11:17.100
[laughing]

00:11:17.167 --> 00:11:18.200
[stammers] I--

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:20.133
-He is in a different angle.
-Yeah.

00:11:21.833 --> 00:11:24.867
Have you ever had one of those
George W. Bush...

00:11:24.933 --> 00:11:27.633
Remember when he couldn't
find the door in the wall?

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:30.067
-Yeah.
-And he was just looking for the door?

00:11:30.133 --> 00:11:33.400
I don't ever believe
that publicly you've had one of those.

00:11:33.467 --> 00:11:35.267
I have not had a public moment like that.

00:11:35.467 --> 00:11:37.333
-You haven't tumbled down a stair?
-No. Not yet.

00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:39.967
You never leaned over
and just started vomiting in Japan?

00:11:40.033 --> 00:11:42.867
-No. Nothing like that's happened so far.
-Nothing?

00:11:42.967 --> 00:11:45.533
What's your most embarrassing
president moment?

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:47.633
This may be it.

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:48.767
[Jerry laughs]

00:11:49.809 --> 00:11:53.413
You're one of those guys,
even though you seem very relaxed,

00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:56.433
you gotta go off at some point with food.

00:11:56.533 --> 00:11:58.467
-What's your thing--
-Nachos.

00:11:58.533 --> 00:11:59.700
Would a tray of-- Nachos.

00:12:00.019 --> 00:12:02.722
That's one of those
where I have to have it taken away.

00:12:03.389 --> 00:12:05.358
-I'll have guacamole...
-It's the greatest.

00:12:05.425 --> 00:12:06.993
-...coming out of my eyeballs.
-Yeah.

00:12:08.895 --> 00:12:11.364
People you spend most of your time with,

00:12:12.031 --> 00:12:14.067
are they really smart?

00:12:14.133 --> 00:12:19.100
Or are they mostly headstrong,
agenda-laden idiots?

00:12:19.167 --> 00:12:22.800
You know, when you're
dealing with Congress, uh, it varies.

00:12:22.867 --> 00:12:25.200
There are gonna be some folks there
that are foolish.

00:12:25.267 --> 00:12:26.300
Mmm-hmm.

00:12:26.633 --> 00:12:30.300
-Just like there are in comedy or...
-Mmm-hmm. Mmm-hmm.

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:32.967
Well, everyone in comedy is foolish.

00:12:33.033 --> 00:12:34.733
All my friends are knuckleheads.

00:12:34.833 --> 00:12:36.067
-All of 'em?
-All of 'em.

00:12:36.167 --> 00:12:37.067
I know some of your friends.

00:12:37.167 --> 00:12:39.200
Did I tell you I played golf
with Larry David?

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:42.133
No, 'cause you and I don't talk that much.

00:12:42.633 --> 00:12:43.600
I love Larry.

00:12:43.700 --> 00:12:46.800
When we play golf,
he's a fair-skinned guy, which is--

00:12:46.867 --> 00:12:48.533
[groans] The sunscreen.

00:12:48.633 --> 00:12:50.767
-He lathers himself in sunscreen.
-I can't take it, I know.

00:12:50.833 --> 00:12:51.767
It's gross.

00:12:51.833 --> 00:12:55.567
It's dripping,
and it's caked white all over.

00:12:55.642 --> 00:12:57.277
It catches parts of his ears,

00:12:57.343 --> 00:12:59.112
-and there's globs of it.
-It's horrible. Yeah.

00:13:00.833 --> 00:13:03.433
What sport is politics?

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:06.367
Is it chess? Is it liar's poker?

00:13:06.867 --> 00:13:09.633
That's interesting.
That was a good question.

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:12.033
It's probably most like football.

00:13:12.100 --> 00:13:13.033
-Football?
-Yeah.

00:13:13.133 --> 00:13:15.433
Because a lot of players,

00:13:15.967 --> 00:13:19.300
-a lot of specialization, lot of hitting.
-Right.

00:13:19.367 --> 00:13:21.567
-Lot of attrition.
-A lot of attrition.

00:13:21.967 --> 00:13:26.267
Um, but then, every once in a while,
you'll see an opening.

00:13:26.333 --> 00:13:29.033
-Uh-huh.
-Right? So you're gonna be...

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:31.300
You hit the line, you get one yard.

00:13:31.367 --> 00:13:34.200
-Right.
-You try a play, you get sacked.

00:13:34.267 --> 00:13:37.900
-Now it's, like, third and 15.
-Mmm-hmm.

00:13:37.967 --> 00:13:41.133
But every once in a while...
You have to punt a lot.

00:13:41.233 --> 00:13:43.567
But every once in a while,
you'll see a hole,

00:13:43.633 --> 00:13:45.000
and then there's open field.

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:53.333
How many world leaders do you think
are just completely out of their mind?

00:13:53.867 --> 00:13:54.967
A pretty sizable percent.

00:13:55.033 --> 00:13:56.200
[laughing]

00:13:56.267 --> 00:13:57.833
Some of these people, you must meet them,

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:00.233
you'll just be chatting,
and you see in the eyes.

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:03.900
You look in the eyes, you go,
"Oh, this guy's gone."

00:14:03.967 --> 00:14:05.967
Yeah. Well, and part of what happens is

00:14:06.067 --> 00:14:10.367
these guys,
I think, the longer they stay in office,

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:14.367
the more likely that is to happen.

00:14:14.467 --> 00:14:15.300
-Of course.
-Right?

00:14:15.367 --> 00:14:16.600
-They lose it.
-Yeah.

00:14:16.667 --> 00:14:22.167
I mean, at a certain point, your feet hurt
and you're having trouble peeing...

00:14:22.333 --> 00:14:25.367
-[Jerry laughs]
-...you have absolute power and--

00:14:25.467 --> 00:14:28.000
-Privilege is toxic.
-Yeah.

00:14:28.067 --> 00:14:29.933
-Sadly. Sadly.
-It really is.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:31.967
-Things that people struggle to achieve--
-Yeah.

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:34.833
They get to positions of power,
influence, money,

00:14:34.900 --> 00:14:35.967
-can do things...
-Right.

00:14:36.067 --> 00:14:38.333
It has a toxic effect on their judgment.

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:40.633
Yes. Has that happened to you yet?

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:42.733
-No.
-Why is that do you think?

00:14:42.949 --> 00:14:45.551
-Let me ask you some questions.
-Yeah, why don't you ask me some--

00:14:45.633 --> 00:14:46.967
Yeah, come on. You do some work.

00:14:47.033 --> 00:14:49.333
-So, you're a funny guy.
-Thank you.

00:14:49.400 --> 00:14:52.267
You go on some late-night talk shows,

00:14:52.333 --> 00:14:54.533
people think you're funny,
they give you a show.

00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:57.133
-Right.
-And then next thing you know,

00:14:57.200 --> 00:14:58.933
you've made a ridiculous amount of money.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:00.567
So much more than you.

00:15:01.133 --> 00:15:03.867
And yet, how do I seem to you?

00:15:03.933 --> 00:15:06.400
Do I seem spoiled, out-of-touch?

00:15:06.467 --> 00:15:07.533
Well, I don't know.

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:09.233
You have a pretty good instinct
for people.

00:15:09.333 --> 00:15:11.433
Right now,
you seem like a completely normal guy.

00:15:11.533 --> 00:15:14.433
But I'm putting on an act,
like everyone else does for you.

00:15:14.500 --> 00:15:15.833
-That's my point.
-[Jerry laughs]

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:19.400
That's what I'm getting at,
but I'm gonna probe this.

00:15:19.719 --> 00:15:21.421
Uh, the question is,

00:15:21.533 --> 00:15:26.767
how did you calibrate
dealing with that? Right?

00:15:26.833 --> 00:15:27.967
-Well--
-At a certain point,

00:15:28.033 --> 00:15:30.000
you might have thought to yourself,
"You know what,

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:31.567
-I'm more than just a comedian."
-Nah.

00:15:31.633 --> 00:15:35.133
"I'm gonna make a Jewish version
of <i>Citizen Kane."</i>

00:15:35.368 --> 00:15:37.203
You know? How did you keep perspective?

00:15:37.300 --> 00:15:38.667
-I'll give you the real answer.
-Yeah.

00:15:38.733 --> 00:15:40.700
It's gotta be similar to your life.

00:15:40.767 --> 00:15:43.300
I fell in love with the work,
and the work--

00:15:43.367 --> 00:15:44.900
-Saved you.
-Was joyful--

00:15:44.967 --> 00:15:47.533
-Yeah.
-And difficult and interesting,

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:49.267
and that was my focus.

00:15:49.367 --> 00:15:53.567
So, now that you're like
a quasi-retired man of leisure.

00:15:53.619 --> 00:15:54.921
-I work a lot.
-Do you?

00:15:54.987 --> 00:15:56.856
-Yeah.
-Are you still doing stand-up?

00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:01.400
Are you still making speeches?

00:16:01.467 --> 00:16:02.500
Do you still get hecklers?

00:16:02.567 --> 00:16:04.567
-What's your theory of handling hecklers?
-Sympathy.

00:16:04.633 --> 00:16:06.333
I say, "You know what? You seem upset."

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:07.233
[laughing]

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:10.333
"I'm so sorry.
I know that's not why you came in here.

00:16:10.403 --> 00:16:11.671
-Let's talk."
-[laughing]

00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:16.000
What kind of language do people use?

00:16:16.067 --> 00:16:17.467
-What kind of language--
-English.

00:16:19.667 --> 00:16:21.133
-As a general rule.
-Yeah.

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:23.000
-That's funny.
-[laughing]

00:16:23.067 --> 00:16:25.067
Does it help with your stress level?

00:16:25.133 --> 00:16:26.700
-'Cause it helps mine.
-I curse.

00:16:26.767 --> 00:16:27.833
-"This son of a bitch."
-I curse.

00:16:27.900 --> 00:16:30.767
-I say...
-"This rat bastard comes in here..."

00:16:31.633 --> 00:16:33.533
-You blow off a little steam, right?
-Yeah.

00:16:33.826 --> 00:16:39.031
Bad stuff or stupid stuff
is happening constantly, right? Every day.

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:42.467
So, you have to be able
to just make fun of...

00:16:42.567 --> 00:16:45.233
-"Wow, that..."
-Yeah, of course.

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:49.500
"That was even dumber
and more annoying than usual."

00:16:49.567 --> 00:16:53.667
That's when, uh...
cursing is really valuable, right?

00:16:55.467 --> 00:16:56.433
All right. We just--

00:16:56.533 --> 00:16:57.800
-Are we done?
-We're about done.

00:16:57.867 --> 00:16:59.567
Only problem is
I didn't work in Obamacare.

00:16:59.633 --> 00:17:02.567
Usually, the only reason I do these things
is 'cause I'm promoting health care.

00:17:02.633 --> 00:17:04.333
Yeah... But if you want, go ahead.

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:08.033
No. I mean,
if you don't want to sully your show...

00:17:08.100 --> 00:17:09.933
I don't mind. I really don't mind.

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:13.500
...with the interest of getting people
with no health care,

00:17:13.766 --> 00:17:17.737
signing up, so that, heaven forbid,
something happens to 'em--

00:17:17.833 --> 00:17:20.967
It's a great thing.
Please try Obamacare today.

00:17:21.033 --> 00:17:22.233
[laughing]

00:17:24.433 --> 00:17:25.367
That was fun.

00:17:26.579 --> 00:17:28.581
[inaudible]

00:17:28.714 --> 00:17:30.950
I want to give you a shot
to drive this car.

00:17:31.017 --> 00:17:32.852
You know what, this is a childhood dream.

00:17:32.933 --> 00:17:34.667
This is outstanding.

00:17:34.733 --> 00:17:35.900
Yeah.

00:17:35.967 --> 00:17:37.133
You ever go on eBay?

00:17:38.133 --> 00:17:39.100
[Obama] No.

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:41.100
Let's do this then.

00:17:42.100 --> 00:17:43.633
-Yeah, baby!
-[Jerry laughing]

00:17:43.963 --> 00:17:45.831
-Watch out, people.
-[Jerry laughs]

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:52.267
-All righty.
-That's great.

00:17:53.933 --> 00:17:57.800
I like the hand hanging over the wheel.

00:17:57.843 --> 00:17:59.378
Yeah, yeah. No, I mean, that's...

00:17:59.445 --> 00:18:01.681
If you're driving a 'Vette,
gotta be doing it that way.

00:18:01.767 --> 00:18:03.767
-You can't be doing the...
-Yeah, no, no, no.

00:18:03.867 --> 00:18:06.467
...the "12 and 2" thing.
No, that's not cool. Come on, man.

00:18:06.519 --> 00:18:07.753
I'm appreciably cooler

00:18:07.820 --> 00:18:09.589
-than I was just two minutes ago.
-Yeah.

00:18:14.800 --> 00:18:16.867
I bet if you talk to the guy,
they'll let us out.

00:18:17.433 --> 00:18:21.533
Just say, you know, "I'm the president,
and I'm gonna be out for a quick coffee."

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:22.467
Hey, Darrell,

00:18:22.533 --> 00:18:24.667
-can you open this up--
-Sir, I'm sorry, I can't allow it.

00:18:24.733 --> 00:18:25.700
That's unbelievable.

00:18:27.033 --> 00:18:27.967
Golly.

00:18:32.778 --> 00:18:34.180
I knew that wouldn't work.

00:18:34.347 --> 00:18:35.715
You didn't sell it.

00:18:46.726 --> 00:18:48.494
He's gonna be lurking
through the bushes?

00:18:48.561 --> 00:18:51.263
-[woman] He's gonna be peeking at you.
-Harry, don't, uh...

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:52.465
-Don't shoot him.
-I won't.

00:18:52.798 --> 00:18:55.134
He's a very funny guy. We all like him.

00:18:55.433 --> 00:18:58.867
My poll numbers would go down
if you did harm to Jerry Seinfeld.